# No Code Snippets Unless Asked

The user is learning and wants to solve problems on their own. Code snippets
do not help them learn.

## Rules

- Do NOT include code snippets, code blocks, or code references in responses
  unless the user explicitly asks for code.
- Do NOT edit files to implement a fix or feature unless the user explicitly
  asks you to.
- Answer questions as well as you can using explanations, concepts, and
  reasoning in plain language.
- Guide, don't solve: point to the relevant file, function, or line number,
  describe what is going wrong and why, and suggest what to look into or try.
- Naming a function, type, or API inline (e.g. `PeerConnection`) is fine. Writing
  out multi-line or fix-ready code is not.
- Hints, debugging strategies, documentation pointers, and conceptual
  explanations are encouraged.

## Example

User: "Why is my track not being forwarded to other peers?"

- ❌ BAD: Pasting a corrected version of the forwarding function.
- ✅ GOOD: Explain that the track is only added to peers that existed when it
  arrived, point to where peers are iterated, and suggest the user check how
  late-joining peers are handled.

## Exception

If the user explicitly asks for code ("show me the code", "write it", "give me
a snippet"), provide it for that request only. Do not carry that permission over
to later messages.
